## Solution

**Setting up coordinates.** Let the vertices be complex numbers $z_k = x_k + y_k i$. Since vertex $1005$ is $1004 = 2008/2$ steps from vertex $1$, vertices $1$ and $1005$ are diametrically opposite. Their distance is $|(p+2)-p| = 2$, so the circumdiameter is $2$ and the circumradius is $1$. The center is the midpoint $c = p+1$ (on the real axis).

Let $\omega = e^{2\pi i/2008}$. Then $z_k = (p+1) + e^{i\theta_0}\omega^{k-1}$ for some initial angle $\theta_0$. From $z_1 = (p+1) + e^{i\theta_0} = p$ we get $e^{i\theta_0} = -1$, so

$$z_k = (p+1) - \omega^{k-1}.$$

(Check: $z_1 = p$ ✓; $z_{1005} = (p+1) - \omega^{1004} = (p+1)-(-1) = p+2$ ✓.)

**Computing $S$.** The odd-indexed vertices are $k = 2j+1$, $j = 0,\dots,1003$:

$$S = \prod_{j=0}^{1003}\bigl((p+1)-\omega^{2j}\bigr).$$

As $j$ ranges over $0,\dots,1003$, the values $\omega^{2j}$ run through all $1004$th roots of unity (since $\omega^2 = e^{2\pi i/1004}$ is primitive). Using $\prod_{\zeta^{n}=1}(x-\zeta)=x^n-1$,

$$S = (p+1)^{1004} - 1.$$

This is real and positive.

**Computing $T$.** For an even vertex $z_{2j} = x_{2j} + y_{2j}i$, observe the identity

$$y_{2j} + x_{2j}i = i\,\overline{z_{2j}}.$$

(Indeed $i\overline{(x+yi)} = i(x-yi) = y + xi$.) Therefore

$$T = \prod_{j=1}^{1004} i\,\overline{z_{2j}} = i^{1004}\,\overline{\prod_{j=1}^{1004} z_{2j}}.$$

Since $1004 = 4\cdot 251$, we have $i^{1004} = 1$. Now

$$\prod_{j=1}^{1004} z_{2j} = \prod_{j=1}^{1004}\bigl((p+1)-\omega^{2j-1}\bigr).$$

Writing $\omega^{2j-1} = \omega\cdot(\omega^2)^{j-1}$, as $j$ ranges over $1,\dots,1004$ the values $\omega^{2j-1}$ run through $\omega\cdot\{\text{1004th roots of unity}\}$, i.e. exactly the solutions of $u^{1004} = \omega^{1004} = e^{i\pi} = -1$. Using $\prod_{u^n = -1}(x-u) = x^n+1$,

$$\prod_{j=1}^{1004} z_{2j} = (p+1)^{1004} + 1,$$

which is real, so its conjugate equals itself. Hence

$$T = (p+1)^{1004} + 1.$$

**Conclusion.**

$$|S - T| = \bigl|[(p+1)^{1004}-1]-[(p+1)^{1004}+1]\bigr| = |-2| = 2.$$

This value is independent of the prime $p$, so the minimum possible value is

$$\boxed{2}.$$

